Systems: Memoryless
Instantaneous or zero memory system
A system is called a memoryless system if its output y(t0) depends only on input applied at t0, independent of input applied before or after t0.
• Most systems have memory; thus, output depends on previous inputs. • System must be relaxed or at rest to develop the input-output description. • A system is relaxed if, at time t1, no energy is stored in the system at that instant. (Every system is assumed to be relaxed at t = −∞)
• Under the relaxedness assumption, with operator H, the output can be
y = Hu
